This can happen when an import changes file permissions or Git’s cached file information. **Clear those entries without discarding your real OSB changes.**

Run these commands from your **Git repository folder**.

**1. Refresh Git’s file information:**

```powershell
git update-index --refresh
git status --short
```

This refreshes the index’s cached information without replacing file contents. Files with actual differences can report `needs update`. [git-update-index Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-update-index?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

**2. If the files remain, check for permission changes:**

```powershell
git diff --summary
```

If you see entries such as:

```text
mode change 100644 => 100755 path/to/file
```

run:

```powershell
git config --local core.fileMode false
git update-index --refresh
git status --short
```

This makes this repository ignore executable-permission differences. **Files with real content changes remain listed.** [git-config Documentation](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-config/2.54.0?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Then reopen GitHub Desktop.

**If that doesn’t clear them**, send me the following output for one affected file:

```powershell
git status --short -- 'path/to/file'
git diff --no-ext-diff --no-textconv -- 'path/to/file'
git diff --cached --no-ext-diff --no-textconv -- 'path/to/file'
git ls-files --eol -- 'path/to/file'
```

That will help distinguish staged changes, line endings, and metadata differences. Avoid **Discard all changes** or `git reset --hard`, since you want to preserve the genuine changes from your import.
